--TEST--
`Core\Db\Row::instant` refuses every column that carries no zone of its own, down to one an engine let a program declare `timestamptz`, and it still reads a NULL column as an absence — `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Db\Row::instant
// SQLite holds no zone-carrying column type: `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`'s SQLite half
// folds every `TIMESTAMP` spelling into a zone-*less* reading, so `at` below is
// declared with this member's own type name and is still a `Core\Time\DateTime`.
// That is the column worth pinning — a reader deciding on the declaration
// rather than on the value would answer it, and the moment it answered would be
// a moment nobody wrote down a zone for.
use Core\Db\Row;

var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");
$db->execute(
    "create table pings (at timestamptz not null, seen datetime not null, born date not null, opens time not null, label text not null, missed timestamptz)",
    []
);
$db->execute(
    "insert into pings (at, seen, born, opens, label, missed) values (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)",
    ["2026-09-03 14:05:00", "2026-09-03 14:05:00", "2026-09-03", "09:30:00", "2026-09-03T14:05:00Z", null]
);

array<Row> $one = $db->query("select at, seen, born, opens, label, missed from pings", [])->all();
var $row = $one[0];

// One question asked of every column the table has, so a member answering one
// of them fails here while still looking right on its own line. The last is the
// text that *is* an RFC 3339 rendering: the reading a member parsing the cell
// would find, in a column declared to hold text.
array<string> $columns = ["at", "seen", "born", "opens", "label"];
foreach ($columns as string $column) {
    try {
        $row->instant($column);
        echo "instant over ", $column, ": answered\n";
    } catch (LogicError $stopped) {
        echo "instant over ", $column, ": refused\n";
    }
}

// A NULL column is an absence and not a refusal, which is the one answer this
// member has on an engine like this one — `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`, spec § 18.
?Core\Time\Instant $missed = $row->instant("missed");
if ($missed == null) {
    echo "instant over missed: null\n";
}

// And the row is a readable row: the neighbour that shares its column answers,
// so every refusal above is this member's class check and not a broken read.
?Core\Time\Date $born = $row->date("born");
if ($born != null) {
    echo "date over born: ", $born->format("yyyy-MM-dd"), "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# § 3: a SQLite connection's file path comes from its config block, so
# `db.connect` covers it.
[capabilities.db]
connect = ["main"]

[db.main]
driver = "sqlite"
path = ":memory:"
--EXPECT--
instant over at: refused
instant over seen: refused
instant over born: refused
instant over opens: refused
instant over label: refused
instant over missed: null
date over born: 2026-09-03
